ns the quadrant the input angle lies in, and place the\n42\t * remainder of :math:`x` divided by :math:`\\frac{\\pi}{4}` in the output array\n43\t * :math:`*y` (which consists of two values, the remainder and its tail,\n44\t * hereafter called :math:`y_0` and :math:`y_1`). The return value can be\n45\t * converted to the angle quadrant by taking just its last two bits. If the\n46\t * last two bits are :math:`00`, the input value is represented in\n47\t * [:math:`-45^{\\circ}`, :math:`45^{\\circ}`], if they are :math:`01`, it is in\n48\t * [:math:`45^{\\circ}`, :math:`135^{\\circ}`], if they are :math:`10`, it is in\n49\t * [:math:`135^{\\circ}`, :math:`225^{\\circ}`], and if they are :math:`11`, it\n50\t * is in [:math:`225^{\\circ}`, :math:`315^{\\circ}`]. The last two bits of the\n51\t * return value are the only relevant part. In some cases the return value also\n52\t * equals the number of reductions (by :math:`\\frac{\\pi}{2}`) necessary to\n53\t * reduce the value - this is not always the case, as the return value would\n54\t * often overflow due to the limited size of ``int32_t``.\n55\t *\n56\t * ``__rem_pio2_internal`` returns the quadrant the input angle lies in (see\n57\t * ``__rem_pio2`` on how to convert the return value into the quadrant), and\n58\t * place the remainder of :math:`x` (scaled :math:`x` which consists of up to\n59\t * three values, hereafter called :math:`x_0`, :math:`x_1` and :math:`x_2`,\n60\t * original :math:`x = x_0 \\cdot 2^{e0} + x_1 \\cdot 2^{e0-24} + x_2 \\cdot\n61\t * 2^{e0-48}`) divided by :math:`\\frac{\\pi}{4}` in the output array (which\n62\t * consists of two values, the remainder and its tail,  :math:`y_0` and\n63\t * :math:`y_1`). The variable :math:`nx` needs to be provided with the size of\n64\t * the array for :math:`x`.\n65\t *\n66\t * Although ``__rem_pio2`` and ``__rem_pio2_internal`` seem to do the same,\n67\t * they work on different ranges. ``__rem_pio2`` first checks if the input is\n68\t * rather close to the t